1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,160
Tuesday, eleven at night.

2
00:00:02,210 --> 00:00:08,448
Bekzod is connecting Malika's flower shop to the bank, and one test payment keeps failing.

3
00:00:08,498 --> 00:00:13,512
To see why, he prints the whole request into a log file, token and all.

4
00:00:13,562 --> 00:00:22,800
In Lesson 19, his server traded the shop's secret for a short-lived token, and with that token it asks ecom to create payments.

5
00:00:22,850 --> 00:00:27,816
This lesson is about why that token alone should never be enough.

6
00:00:27,866 --> 00:00:29,760
A token says who you are.

7
00:00:29,810 --> 00:00:33,168
It does not say that you wrote this particular request.

8
00:00:33,218 --> 00:00:36,672
And tokens leak, exactly like this one.

9
00:00:36,722 --> 00:00:43,416
Bekzod's log ends up in a support chat, and anyone who reads it now holds a working token.

10
00:00:43,466 --> 00:00:50,232
With it, they can ask for a payment of any amount, and send the buyer back to any address they like.

11
00:00:50,282 --> 00:00:52,104
There is a second danger.

12
00:00:52,154 --> 00:00:57,600
Someone who records one honest request can simply send it again tomorrow.

13
00:00:57,650 --> 00:00:59,736
Most teams stop at the token.

14
00:00:59,786 --> 00:01:05,688
I think that is the most common mistake in payment APIs, and the cheapest one to avoid.

15
00:01:05,738 --> 00:01:13,424
What can Bekzod add to a request, so that a stolen token is worth nothing?

16
00:01:13,474 --> 00:01:16,904
So Bekzod's server seals every request.

17
00:01:16,954 --> 00:01:19,736
That seal is the request signature.

18
00:01:19,786 --> 00:01:22,568
It covers the method, which says create.

19
00:01:22,618 --> 00:01:26,288
It covers the full address the request goes to.

20
00:01:26,338 --> 00:01:28,160
It covers the token.

21
00:01:28,210 --> 00:01:33,944
It covers a fingerprint of the body, so the amount and the return address are sealed too.

22
00:01:33,994 --> 00:01:36,920
And it covers the time and a one-time number.

23
00:01:36,970 --> 00:01:40,640
Change any of them, and the seal no longer fits.

24
00:01:40,690 --> 00:01:43,232
The seal is made with a signing key.

25
00:01:43,282 --> 00:01:50,936
That key is a secret held by only two parties: the bank and this one API client.

26
00:01:50,986 --> 00:01:54,392
The bank writes it straight into its key safe.

27
00:01:54,442 --> 00:02:00,416
The payments app can read it, but never change it, and no person ever types it into the app.

28
00:02:00,466 --> 00:02:04,904
When a request arrives, ecom checks it in a fixed order.

29
00:02:04,954 --> 00:02:10,112
First, the time must be fresh, inside the bank's chosen window.

30
00:02:10,162 --> 00:02:13,712
Second, the body must match its fingerprint.

31
00:02:13,762 --> 00:02:20,000
Third, the API client named in the seal must be the one that owns the token.

32
00:02:20,050 --> 00:02:25,232
Last, ecom makes the seal again with the same key and compares the two.

33
00:02:25,282 --> 00:02:28,040
Only then does it spend the one-time number.

34
00:02:28,090 --> 00:02:32,120
That number is the nonce, and each nonce works once.

35
00:02:32,170 --> 00:02:39,080
Because it is spent only after the seal checks out, a forger cannot burn Malika's numbers in advance.

36
00:02:39,130 --> 00:02:40,976
One more rule follows.

37
00:02:41,026 --> 00:02:48,128
A payment can be taken only for Malika's own website, switched on, with one of its own return addresses.

38
00:02:48,178 --> 00:02:51,704
Wednesday morning, Bekzod tries it properly.

39
00:02:51,754 --> 00:02:58,856
His server builds the body: the website, the order, the amount in cents and the return address.

40
00:02:58,906 --> 00:03:01,760
It seals the request and sends it.

41
00:03:01,810 --> 00:03:03,824
The answer comes back at once.

42
00:03:03,874 --> 00:03:09,560
The payment is created, and the answer carries the link to the bank's payment page.

43
00:03:09,610 --> 00:03:15,704
In the merchant portal, Malika's payments list shows one new payment, waiting for the buyer.

44
00:03:15,754 --> 00:03:18,296
Then Bekzod plays the attacker.

45
00:03:18,346 --> 00:03:23,456
He sends the very same sealed request a second time, unchanged.

46
00:03:23,506 --> 00:03:27,992
The bank refuses it, because the one-time number is already used.

47
00:03:28,042 --> 00:03:33,056
He copies a request, changes the amount, and keeps the old seal.

48
00:03:33,106 --> 00:03:37,112
The bank refuses it, because the body no longer matches.

49
00:03:37,162 --> 00:03:39,632
He sends a request sealed yesterday.

50
00:03:39,682 --> 00:03:44,144
The bank refuses that too, because it is no longer fresh.

51
00:03:44,194 --> 00:03:49,448
After every refusal, the payments list stays exactly the same.

52
00:03:49,498 --> 00:03:51,656
Nothing new is created.

53
00:03:51,706 --> 00:03:55,664
So the token in that support chat is worth nothing on its own.

54
00:03:55,714 --> 00:04:00,032
Without the seal, it would be a blank cheque in Malika's name.

55
00:04:00,082 --> 00:04:06,032
Without the nonce, one recorded request could be sent a thousand times.

56
00:04:06,082 --> 00:04:10,208
The seal protects her name and every number in the request.

57
00:04:10,258 --> 00:04:13,400
The time and the nonce protect against copies.

58
00:04:13,450 --> 00:04:16,664
And the key safe protects the key itself.

59
00:04:16,714 --> 00:04:23,072
To recap: the token says who you are, and the seal says this exact request is yours.

60
00:04:23,122 --> 00:04:28,120
So what makes the leaked token worth nothing?

61
00:04:28,170 --> 00:04:31,912
The seal, with its time and its one-time number.

62
00:04:31,962 --> 00:04:38,248
Next: what happens when Bekzod's server honestly sends the same payment twice.
